<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_lrc2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>RLC Circuits</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in a 1-hour interactive classroom, after students have completed the rest of the chapter:
	</p>
  </assemblage>



<activity>
	<title>Extending the Analogy</title>
	<introduction>
		<p></p>
	<p>
		The two situations shown below can be modeled using the same mathematics!
	</p>
	<figure xml:id="fig_LRC2">
					<caption>An RLC circuit diagram with a voltage source.</caption>
					<image source="LRC2.png" width = "80%" ><description><p>A circuit with a battery, an open switch, a resistor, an inductor, and a capacitor in series.</p></description></image>
				</figure>
	<figure xml:id="fig_LRC-block">
					<caption>A block on a spring with drag and pushing forces.</caption>
					<image source="BlockSpring1.png" width = "80%" ><description><p>A block is connected to a spring, subject to a drag force and a pushing force.</p></description></image>
				</figure>
	</introduction>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
		Identify the aspect of the block-spring-drag system that corresponds to each circuit element.
	</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
		<p>
		The two situations can be analogized by writing parallel equations using the Law of Motion for the block on a spring and the Loop Rule for the RLC circuit:
		<md>F - kx - bv - ma = 0</md>
		<md>F - kx - b\frac{dx}{dt} - m\frac{d^2x}{dt^2} = 0</md>
		<md>V - \frac{Q}{C} - IR - L\frac{dI}{dt} = 0</md>
		<md>V - \frac{Q}{C} - R\frac{dQ}{dt} - L\frac{d^2Q}{dt^2} = 0</md>
		Identifying term by term gives the resistor is dissipative (like drag), the inductor resists change (like mass/inertia), the capacitor stores energy (like the spring) and the battery provides voltage (like a pushing force).
		<md>b \rightarrow R</md>
		<md>m \rightarrow L</md>
		<md>k \rightarrow \frac{1}{C}</md>
		<md>F \rightarrow V</md>
	</p>
	</solution>	
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Supposing that the spring begins at equilibrium, the block begins at rest, and you push with a <alert>constant force</alert>, describe the resulting motion of the block.  How could you push differently to achieve different motion?
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Interestingly, the constant pushing force makes very little difference to the analysis (it is equivalent to shifting the origin), so the position as a function of time is described by <xref ref="assumption-damped"/>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Replacing each quantity in the equations above with the circuits analogy gives the following:
			</p>
			<assumption xml:id="assumption-lrc">
  <title>RLC Circuit</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
    </p>
    <p>
      The charge on a capacitor in an RLC circuit is given by

	  <md> Q(t) = Q_{\text{max}} e^{-Rt/2L} \cos{(\omega_{RLC} t + \phi_o)}</md>
      
		The oscillation frequency is given by the following expression.

		<md> \omega_{RLC} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{LC} - \frac{R^2}{4L^2}}</md>

		Note that <m>\omega_{RLC}</m> is only valid when <m>\frac{1}{LC} \gt \frac{R^2}{4L^2}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
		The current may be found by taking a derivative, though it is convenient to rewrite it in terms of a different phase angle:
		<md> I(t) = I_{\text{max}} \cos{(\omega_{RLC} t + \phi_I)}</md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</assumption>
		</solution>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				What happens if you <q>push</q> with a non-constant voltage: an alternating voltage?  Predict whether the shape of the graph is more likely to look like the <xref ref="damped_pos"/> or the <xref ref="HM_position"/>.  Why?
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				After some time, the driving voltage wins, giving a simpler functional form for the current:
				<md>I(t) \rightarrow I_{max}\cos\left(\omega t - \phi\right)</md>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Try building this circuit in the <q>RLC</q> tab of the <url href="https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/circuit-construction-kit-ac/latest/circuit-construction-kit-ac_all.html">Circuits PhET simulation</url> to check your predictions.  Good numbers to try are given below.
				<md>V_o = 25 \mathrm{~V}</md>
				<md>R = 3 \mathrm{~\Omega}</md>
				<md>L = 5 \mathrm{~H}</md>
				<md>C = 0.1 \mathrm{~F}</md>
				<md>f = 0.5 \mathrm{~Hz}</md>
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				What could you do to the circuit at this point to get <alert>more current</alert>?  Try to come up with as many different ways as you can.  Check your answers using the <url href="https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/circuit-construction-kit-ac/latest/circuit-construction-kit-ac_all.html">Circuits PhET simulation</url>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				An obvious change to try is decreasing the resistance.  However, you can also adjust the inductance, the capacitance, or the frequency.  A good thing to try in the simulation is slowly lowering the inductance from <m>5 \mathrm{~H}</m> to <m>1 \mathrm{~H}</m> and then finally to <m>0.1 \mathrm{~H}</m>.  The maximum current will increase then decrease, an example of resonance!  You can find the frequency at which the current is maximized using the equation below:
				<md>I_{max} = \frac{V_o}{\sqrt{R^2 + \left( \omega L - \frac{1}{\omega C} \right)^2}}</md>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</task>
</activity>



	
	

<!--NO AI-->
</section>

